  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Fog and low stratus will impact travel conditions through\n   Wednesday day across northwest Montana\n\n-  Remnants of an atmospheric river will begin to move in by\n   Thanksgiving morning causing snow levels to rise above passes\n   initially then changing back to wet snow at night. \n\n-  Wet weather with pass-level and higher elevation valley winter\n   impacts later Friday and Friday night. \n\n-  Brief respite from precipitation this weekend though fog and\n   low clouds continue to be possible. A Warm front to bring \n   another round of precipitation Sunday night into Monday.\n\nSatellite and radar trends show cloud cover and light\nprecipitation moving towards northern Idaho this afternoon. There\ncould be some light rain along the Idaho/Montana border developing\nlater tonight through Wednesday morning. Other than that, high \npressure will keep more stable weather with low clouds or fog at\ntimes across northwest and west-central Montana.\n\nThe models have trended a little slower with the arrival of the\natmospheric river, generally arriving by Thursday morning from Elk\nRiver, Idaho to the Lincoln County, Montana. As the plume of\nmoisture moves over the remainder of the region, snow levels will\nrise with the influx of milder air aloft. Some mountain passes may\njust see a quick changeover to rain with minimal snow\naccumulation. By Friday morning, the mountains along the\nIdaho/Montana border and Clearwater Mountains could receive \nbetween 0.75\" and 1.50\" of precipitation, mostly in the form of \nrain below 5,500 feet. Then another 0.50\" to 1.50\" precipitation\nis possible through Saturday morning with snow levels falling to\n4500 to 5000 feet. Friday night could be the more impactful period\nfor the high elevation valley travel in southwest Montana as \ntemperatures will trend towards freezing or below. \n\nThere is a caveat to the Friday system. There are hints that the\nshortwave passage later Friday into Friday night could become more\nimpactful with lower snow levels. We have very low confidence on \nthis low probability, yet potentially more impactful scenario. We'll\nkeep watching the trends and hopefully by Wednesday or Thursday, \nwe will have more detail. \n\nFor Saturday into Sunday morning, the chance for precipitation is\nlow as a transient upper ridge moves over the region. It could be\nmore of a \"dirty\" ridge in that there will be more Pacific\nmoisture starting to move through it during the day Sunday. A warm\nfront could bring a brief period of snow to lower elevations \nSunday night before transitioning to rain. Mountain passes could\nhave wintry impacts at times.",
